The [original incident](/kb/incidents/4471/) is a 4 October snapshot: it calls this one known issue with one fix queued. The reports describe a parser defect, a booking-form hint defect and a separate set of one-day moves. Engineering has since closed #4471, but that does not establish that every problem in it is fixed.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering update on the [Challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows), read on 28 October: #4471 closed 05 Oct. Parser: FIXED, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. Hint: WON'T FIX, `booking-v3 form retired`. Neither ending accounts for a date moving by one day; that problem stays open.
</Callout>

## What the code and data show

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table runs the supplied `uniqueById`, `groupByCause`, `causeOf` and tenant directory. It does not group by subject or trust the browser's locale. `meta.intakeTag` records what intake guessed when the report arrived.

### Shared parser: month first instead of tenant order

The shared parser reads every date month first, while the affected tenants write day first. An ambiguous date can be interpreted as a different month and day. For example, `R-41200` reports April and July being swapped at `harbour.dental`, whose locale is `en-GB` and whose form is `booking-v2`.

The desk files the noted one-day descriptions under `parser`, so the original accounting count is preserved. That assignment is not a diagnosis. The parser issue is now closed as completed because engineering reports `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`; the one-day descriptions have their own open issue below. We have not independently tested the production patch.

### Booking-v3: the hint and the form disagree

For a day-first tenant using `booking-v3`, a report arriving through `form` follows a different mechanism: the hint says `MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads the input in the tenant's order. A user following the hint can therefore create the wrong date. `R-41230` is a `copper.studio` form report; the tenant uses `es-ES` and `booking-v3`, despite intake tagging it as `parser`.

The hint issue is closed as not planned: engineering retired booking-v3 instead of repairing that form. This is a different outcome from the parser fix; neither outcome closes the one-day problem.

## Reconcile the export and keep the evidence

<ReviewEvidence />

## Incoming reports are not merge activity

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Read the two series separately: `receivedAt` tracks intake, while `mergedAt` tracks the desk's actions. A bulk merge can make an old problem look new if we use the latter as its onset. The export establishes the first observed report, not the moment the software defect was introduced.

## Replace subject-only triage

The [old triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) and [accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/) instruct the desk to merge matching subjects. Their 2024 review date is beyond the kb's staleness threshold. A status of `accepted` does not make them current.

The existing explorer demonstrates why the wording fails. The same subject can describe different mechanisms when the tenant or channel changes. Different subjects can share a cause. Unknown tenants must remain unresolved rather than being merged by guesswork.

<DedupeExplorer />

The proposed [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) replaces subject-only triage with cause-based grouping. Decision 0001 remains unchanged as historical evidence; this review and the new proposal do not claim the desk has adopted it.

## Handoff and next review

The three problem tickets and their outcomes:

- [Parser, issue 1](https://github.com/patrykmurzyn/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1): completed, engineering reports the parser fix.
- [Booking-v3 hint, issue 2](https://github.com/patrykmurzyn/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2): not planned, the form was retired.
- [One-day moves, issue 3](https://github.com/patrykmurzyn/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3): open, no reported remedy covers these descriptions.

The old incident page and accepted decision remain historical records. This signed review reconciles the export with the later desk update, rather than treating investigation completion as a software fix.

- Follow up the one-day problem using report IDs and the actual observed dates, including both participants' time zones. The technical root cause is still unconfirmed.
- On a fresh export, reject conflicting copies of an ID and regenerate the evidence and tickets together.
- Before merging a new report, distinguish a day/month swap from a one-day move; a matching subject is insufficient.
- Verify each remedy against its own reproduction cases. Closing the merged parent ticket does not resolve every child problem.

